e different parts right think  about all of your organs and the various  you know systems that you've got you  know nervous system circulatory system  there's a lot of as we say moving parts  and all those parts have to cooperate  with each other or else there's going to  be problems so you know sometimes if one  part works harder than it should that  can cause problems for all the other  parts so in a certain sense health is  this this state of a kind of equilibrium  in which things are all going the way  that they're supposed to the physical  processes are working the way that they  should what health has another aspect to  so it's it's good love but it also  involves good desires so if you want to  stay healthy and your body does want to  stay healthy there are certain things  that you need to keep doing like eating  and drinking and you know getting some  exercise and perhaps sunbathing you know  if the ozone layer isn't depleted like  it is for us all these sorts of things  right there there are certain limits  within which one would would you know do  these sorts of things you don't just eat  everything whatsoever all the time or  you're going to be unhealthy that leads  us to talking about disease so disease  is the opposite of Health and notice  that opposites are going to play an  incredibly important role for Eric  Symmachus not only at the sort of bottom  basis level but at what we might call  metal levels or organizational levels  where its entire complexes of things  that are that are opposed to each other  fundamentally but are either brought  into some sort of harmony with each  other or are you know in some sort of  discord with each other in any case  disease represents a state of the body  in which the body itself is a unity but  it's not a unity that is working well  it's not a unity that is entirely  harmonious it's divided against itself  things are not working as they should  you know if we think about this we're  thinking about Plato here if we think  about this in Psychological terms then  we would have play those parts of the  soul that going  you know combat each other until they're  properly harmonized here Eric Symmachus  is thinking about the organs of the body  and the things that compose those organs  now of course they didn't have a you  know a cellular theory at that time they  thought of the organs as being composed  of some matter that was roughly  understood in terms of you know basic  elements and we're gonna we're gonna get  to that account in just a moment in any  case what's important to think about  about disease as opposed to health is  that disease not only represents a  disordered condition of the body it also  introduces in the person who feel who is  diseased new desires which are unhealthy  desires diseased desires so the person  who has you know there's this disease  that they call dropsy where you would  just keep drinking you know water over  and over again because you kept on  feeling thirsty and eventually you would  you would die of that that's a bad  desire to have because it's not going to  you know produce health in the body so  when you're diseased you don't think  about the sort of things that you want  to do and you're feeling sick you just  want to lay around do nothing if you  have those desires all the time that  would be a problem right so there's  opposition's between health and disease  not only in the way that they're  composed or or decomposed but also in  the desires that they have and although  Erikson MCUs doesn't make this  completely clear we can also think of  desires in a sense of producing health  or producing disease right not only does  does being healthy tend to give you good  desires to want to remain healthy it  also is a product of following good  desires not eating too much but eating a  certain amount not drinking too much but  drinking a certain amount we could talk  in terms of mental health you know not  going out and doing every single  possible activity but doing some  activities having some desire for social  interaction likewise there are some  desires that are going to be unhealthy  in the  in that they not only tend to be the  things that that people who are  unhealthy feel and are motivated by but  if you if you're healthy and then you  have those sort of desires that are  going to lead to disease sooner or later  you're not going to be healthy so you  know think about here's an example not  one that Eric Simic gets gifts but one  that would work very well for this think  about what goes on with addiction too  you know particularly pleasurable drugs  you you know you try out something and  you find that while this really feels  good and now you've got this desire and  the question is is this a desire that's  going to lead to health or is this a  desire that's gonna lead to disease  you know I'll use one that I can relate  to quite well which is nicotine right or  well caffeine is another great example -  it's not good for you to drink two pots  of coffee a day which is something that  I often do but it's pleasurable is that  good for the body over over time and you  know it's it's something that's probably  conducing to some level of disease right  makes it harder to sleep at night you  know we could run through all sorts of  things so you know if you are originally  healthy and then you follow out an  unhealthy desire sooner or later you can  end up being diseased you know the flip  side of that you could say that part of  what we try to do is steering people  away from diseases to health is to get  them to do the sorts of things that will  actually make healthy life desirable for  them the inculcate new desires and Eric  Seneca says that what the the art of  medicine consists in he tells us to two  sets of things that are important one I  put here the ins and outs of the body  all right he talks about you know  filling and repletion right we might say  you know putting things in the body  takin